# Caddy's access log, on its own so it can be rotated on its own schedule.
#
# Split out of the job-log config on 2026-09-28. Both lived in one file, and
# the hourly timer added that morning was therefore rotating the job logs
# hourly too -- which fragmented them and left `brawlzone-log tiktok` showing
# an empty current file while the run it was meant to show sat in an archive.
#
# This file is what `brawlzone-logrotate.timer` runs hourly. The job logs stay
# with the daily system run, where they belong: they come to a few hundred
# kilobytes a day between them.
#
# `maxsize` is the whole reason for the hourly cadence. The access log is a
# line per request, so its growth rate is set by whoever is pointed at the site
# rather than by anything this box does. 2026-09-28: a scraper flood produced
# 4.6 GB in seven hours while `maxsize 100M` sat in this config doing nothing,
# because logrotate only evaluates maxsize when logrotate runs, and it ran once
# a day. A full disk is how Postgres died on 2026-09-09.
#
# copytruncate because Caddy holds the file open; it appends rather than
# seeking, so truncation is safe. No `delaycompress`: Caddy reopens on
# rotation, and a rotated file sitting uncompressed for a day is what turned
# 489 MB into a disk emergency once already.
#
# Seven days. A crawler refused at 81 requests a second is still a line per
# request -- 250-330 MB compressed per day -- and thirty of those is a fifth of
# the disk for a file nobody reads past a week.
/var/log/brawlzone/access/*.log {
    daily
    maxsize 100M
    rotate 7
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    copytruncate
    dateext
    dateformat -%Y-%m-%d-%s
    su root adm
    create 0664 root adm
}
